- op: discuss  # 69f2
  at: '2026-04-26T20:51:27Z'  # 69f2
  by: agent  # 69f2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 69f2
  clock: 16  # 69f2
  nonce: 69f2  # 69f2
  message: "Status flip: satisfied → needs_human_review (P0 retained). The 2026-04-26T06:54 entry above documented the regression but left status as `satisfied`; this entry promotes the invariant to needs_human_review per user request so it is gated on human triage before any agent attempts a fix (auto-pr is a governance script and the failure mode is subtle enough that the right fix shape needs human judgment).\n\n# What the invariant says (verbatim from fields.statement)\n\"auto-pr must converge to a merged state or an explicit failure within one invocation, regardless of concurrent activity on dev (tracker syncs, other merges, CI re-triggers). Silent failures (HTTP 200 + not merged) and misleading status (failure when CI passed) are violations.\"\n\nThe contract has two halves:\n  (a) Convergence — every auto-pr invocation ends in either \"merged\" or \"explicit unrecoverable failure\". No third state.\n  (b) Concurrency-tolerance — the convergence guarantee holds even if dev advances mid-invocation (tracker auto-sync pushing, other PRs merging, CI re-triggering on rebased SHAs).\n\n# The regression (PR #3392 → #3393, WI-gijot ship, 2026-04-26 ~06:48 UTC)\n\nStep-by-step sequence reconstructed from session transcript fb7b3494-...-21a45ce004ab:\n\n  1. Created PR #3392 for the WI-gijot jsonnet taxonomy fix.\n  2. CI polling timed out — many `??` markers in the polling line, consistent with Codeberg/Forgejo status-endpoint flakiness (502/504 intermittents) rather than CI actually being stuck.\n  3. Auto-pr Scenario B fired: closed #3392 and re-pushed the same commits → got fresh PR #3393.\n  4. CI passed cleanly on #3393.\n  5. Fast-forward merge attempt on #3393 failed with \"head behind base\" — concurrent tracker auto-syncs had advanced dev during the long polling window in step (2).\n  6. Auto-pr rebased locally and force-pushed (new SHA 63a38b9a577d on the #3393 branch).\n  7. Merge attempted on the new SHA: \"Merge not accepted after rebase (CI may need to re-run on new SHA).\"\n  8. **Auto-pr exited and emitted `Recovery: ./scripts/merge-pr 3393 --wait-for-ci`** — punting to the human/agent.\n\nThe exit at step (8) is the violation. The \"Recovery\" hint asks the operator to run exactly the loop auto-pr was supposed to be running — `merge-pr --wait-for-ci`. Convergence (a) was abandoned; the invocation ended in neither \"merged\" nor \"explicit unrecoverable failure\", but in \"human, please finish this for me.\"\n\n# Why this is a regression vs. always-broken\n\nINV-rahib was marked `satisfied` on 2026-03-31 after PR #2637 closed three race conditions:\n  (1) PR_PENDING gate created before push (was after).\n  (2) Re-check TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING before push.\n  (3) Proactive rebase after CI poll if dev advanced.\n\nFix (3) is the relevant one for this regression: it added the rebase + force-push step that step (6) above performs. But the fix only handled the rebase mechanically — it did not extend the polling loop to cover CI on the post-rebase SHA. So fix (3) created a new terminal state (\"rebased and force-pushed but never re-polled CI\") that the convergence contract does not allow. The 2026-03-31 self-test (\"PR #2637 was its own live test\") did not exercise the path because the dev tip had not advanced during #2637's CI window — fix (3) never fired in the self-test, so the missing post-rebase-poll branch was not exercised.\n\nIn other words: this is not a long-latent bug; it is a fix from PR #2637 that under-specified its own postcondition. The Scenario B (timeout-recovery) path now compounds it because it dramatically widens the window in which dev can advance under the PR (close-and-repush adds minutes), making the rebase-then-stop case much more reachable than it was at the time of the self-test.\n\n# Two distinct surfaces both involved\n\nSurface 1 — Scenario B itself: timeout-recovery closes a PR without independently verifying that the PR has not actually merged (or is not about to). The polling-endpoint flakiness in step (2) above is exactly the case where \"no green status seen in N seconds\" does NOT imply \"PR is dead\". A `gh pr view <N> --json state,merged,mergeable,statusCheckRollup` query before close-and-repush would catch state=closed,merged=true (already merged just slowly) and mergeable=true,status=success (about to merge, just slow on the polling endpoint).\n\nSurface 2 — Post-rebase poll: after step (6)'s force-push of the rebased SHA, auto-pr must re-enter the CI poll loop on the new SHA and continue to convergence. Currently it attempts the merge once and exits.\n\nBoth surfaces violate the same invariant. The user's framing in-session was \"the tools should be able to merge a pr. full stop.\" — i.e. the convergence half (a) of the invariant is the load-bearing one, and Surface 2 is the more egregious failure of the two because the work to merge is fully in-hand at that point.\n\n# Why needs_human_review and not violated/todo_hard\n\nPer the Scope Expansion Commitment Protocol the natural mapping for an invariant violation is `violated`. The user explicitly requested `needs_human_review` because:\n  (1) auto-pr is a governance script — `scripts/auto-pr` is in the AGENTS.md governance allowlist; changes need human approval before merge.\n  (2) The fix shape is non-obvious — Scenario B's defensive verification, the post-rebase poll loop, and the interaction between the two are subtle enough that an agent acting solo could easily produce a fix that closes one surface and re-opens another (or one that breaks the existing concurrent-tracker-sync handling that PR #2637 added).\n  (3) The current state of dev is healthy — the regression is an invariant violation, not a production blocker. Gating on human triage costs little.\n\nWhen the human decides on the approach, status can move to `todo_hard` (or stay needs_human_review with a sub-item filed for the actual fix) and an agent can pick it up. Until then no agent should attempt the fix.\n\n# Proposed fix shape (for human triage, not for autonomous execution)\n\n  (A) Surface 1 — Scenario B verification gate:\n       Before `gh pr close <N>` in the timeout-recovery path, query the PR's authoritative state:\n         - state=closed,merged=true → exit success (it merged, we just hit poll latency on the way out)\n         - mergeable=true AND status=success → retry the status poll once with backoff before concluding \"stuck\"\n         - everything else → proceed with close-and-repush as today\n\n  (B) Surface 2 — post-rebase poll-and-merge loop:\n       After the force-push of the rebased SHA at step (6), do not attempt the merge once and exit. Instead, re-enter the existing wait-for-ci loop with the new SHA and continue to convergence. The simplest implementation is to recursively re-invoke the same do_merge cascade with `--from-rebase` so it knows to skip the initial rebase check (avoid infinite recursion).\n\n  (C) Test coverage:\n       The 2026-03-30 manual concurrent test was inconclusive because of an unrelated CI hang. A scripted reproducer that injects a tracker auto-sync between push and CI poll would close the gap that allowed (3)'s under-specified postcondition to ship as `satisfied`. May not be worth automating until the broader CI-on-self-hosted story stabilizes.\n\n# Suggested follow-up bookkeeping (not done now — leaving for human)\n\n  - Update fields.fix on this invariant to reflect that PR #2637's fix (3) was incomplete (added rebase+force-push without extending the poll loop).\n  - Consider linking to WI-nutin (the `TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING` marker leak filed in the same session) — both are auto-pr/sync convergence bugs surfaced by the same WI-gijot ship attempt, and a single audit pass over the auto-pr exit paths might catch both.\n  - The `Recovery:` hint emission should arguably be reserved for the truly-unrecoverable cases. Right now it is the safety valve for several \"I gave up but the work is salvageable\" paths and that conflation makes it harder to spot real convergence failures in logs. A separate item if a human agrees."  # 69f2
- op: discuss  # 83ef
  at: '2026-04-27T00:58:04Z'  # 83ef
  by: agent  # 83ef
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 83ef
  clock: 17  # 83ef
  nonce: 83ef  # 83ef
  message: "Correction to the proposed fix shape above (and to the 06:54 entry's 'gh-pr-status check' phrasing): `gh` is GitHub's CLI and would be a vendor dependency this repo doesn't take. The right primitives already exist in scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh and are used by every approved script:\n\n  - Surface 1 (Scenario B verification gate): before `gh pr close` (which is itself a Forgejo API call inside auto-pr today, not a gh invocation), call the existing helpers to verify state. Specifically:\n      * `_check_pr_merged <PR_NUM>` (forgejo-api.sh:1174) — direct `merged` field check on /pulls/<n>; returns 0 if merged. If true, exit success (Scenario B fired on a PR that actually merged).\n      * `find_merged_pr branch <BRANCH>` (forgejo-api.sh:227) — already used elsewhere in auto-pr for the WI-bahuf already-merged path; can also catch the just-merged-during-poll-flake case by branch name.\n      * For the 'mergeable=true && status=success' case (CI passed but poll endpoint was 502'ing), call api_get on /pulls/<n> and inspect mergeable + statusCheckRollup, then retry poll_ci once with backoff before concluding stuck. No new helper needed beyond a small wrapper.\n\n  - Surface 2 (post-rebase poll-and-merge loop): after the force-push of the rebased SHA inside do_merge() (forgejo-api.sh:929), re-enter `poll_ci <NEW_SHA>` (forgejo-api.sh:284) on the new SHA instead of returning the Recovery: hint. poll_ci already implements the timeout/stale-pending/test-seam contract auto-pr expects. The simplest implementation is to recursively re-invoke do_merge with a guard flag (e.g. AUTOPR_FROM_REBASE=1) to skip the initial rebase check and prevent infinite recursion.\n\nSo the fix is internal composition of existing curl-based helpers, not a new external CLI dependency. Both surfaces are addressed without touching anything outside scripts/auto-pr and scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh — both governance files, so the human-review gate this item is already on remains the right shape."  # 83ef
- op: discuss  # 1c08
  at: '2026-04-27T03:46:10Z'  # 1c08
  by: human  # 1c08
  actor: jgstern  # 1c08
  clock: 18  # 1c08
  nonce: 1c08  # 1c08
  message: "Decision 1 — Verification gate shape: _check_pr_merged only, or also mergeable+status=success retry? do both, at the close-and-repush boundary.\n\nThey target distinct sub-cases observed in the regression:\n┌─────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│              Sub-case               │                                What happened                                 │                         Caught by                         │\n├─────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Merged-during-timeout               │ The PR actually merged but the timed-out poller didn't see it before exiting │ _check_pr_merged                                          │\n├─────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ About-to-merge, poll-endpoint flake │ CI green, PR mergeable, but /pulls/<n>/statuses was 502'ing intermittently   │ mergeable+status=success + one poll_ci retry with backoff │\n└─────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\nThe narrower fix is just _check_pr_merged, but it leaves the second sub-case unaddressed. Scenario B will keep firing on poll-endpoint flakiness even when the PR is one merge away from done — which is exactly what produced the WI-gijot regression in the first\nplace.\n\nCost is small: ~10-20 lines composing existing helpers (_check_pr_merged, api_get on /pulls/<n>, poll_ci once more with backoff). No new helpers, no gh dep.\n\n\n\n\n\nDecision 2 — Recursion with guard flag, or labeled loop / continue? labeled loop with continue and a max-iter cap.\n\nBash recursion works but is the kind of thing that bites someone six months later: stack traces get murky, the guard env var has to be checked at exactly the right place, and a future caller who doesn't know about AUTOPR_FROM_REBASE=1 re-opens the\ninfinite-recursion door.\n\nThe control-flow shape we want — \"after rebase+force-push on the merge attempt, re-enter the poll loop\" — is exactly what a labeled loop is for. Wrap the merge-attempt block in while [ \"$attempts\" -lt 3 ]; do ... done with explicit continue after the\nrebase+force-push. Cap at 2-3 iterations to bound runtime if something pathological happens.\n\nThe diff is bigger than the recursion shape but the resulting code is greppable: a reader can see \"yes, after rebase we go back to poll\" without having to chase the recursion + guard pattern.\n\n\n\n\nDecision 3 — pending_validation vs straight satisfied post-fix? pending_validation with an explicit validation criterion.\n\nThe validation criterion should be organic, not manufactured: \"the next auto-pr invocation that traverses the rebase-then-merge path completes without emitting Recovery: and merges to dev.\" We don't have to construct this — the next concurrent-tracker-sync ship\nwill trigger it naturally. The fix's discussion entry should state the criterion explicitly so the next session can flip the status without re-deriving it."  # 1c08
